\id JAS - The Text-Critical English New Testament
\h THE LETTER OF JAMES
\toc1 The Letter of James
\toc2 James
\toc3 James
\mt2 THE LETTER OF
\mt1 JAMES
\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\m
\v 1 James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes who are scattered abroad: Greetings.
\s1 The Testing of Your Faith Produces Endurance
\m
\v 2 Count it all joy, my brothers, when you experience various trials,
\v 3 because you know that the testing of your faith produces endurance.
\v 4 And let endurance have its full effect, so that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing.
\v 5 If any of you lacks wisdom, he should ask God, who gives generously to all without reproach, and it will be given to him.
\v 6 But he must ask in faith without doubting, for he who doubts is like a wave of the sea that is driven by the wind and tossed about.
\v 7 That person should not expect to receive anything from the Lord.
\v 8 A double-minded man is unstable in all his ways.
\s1 The Lowly Brother and the Rich Man
\m
\v 9 The lowly brother should rejoice in being exalted,
\v 10 and the rich man should rejoice in being made low, because he will pass away like a flower of grass.
\v 11 For the sun rises with its scorching heat and withers the grass, and its flower falls, and its beautiful appearance is lost. So also will the rich man fade away in his pursuits.
\s1 Enduring Temptation
\m
\v 12 Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has stood the test, he will receive the crown of life, which \f + \fr 1:12 \fq the Lord \ft 89.1% ¦ he CT 2%\f*the Lord has promised to those who love him.
\v 13 No one should say when he is tempted, “I am being tempted by God,” for God is not tempted by evil, and he himself tempts no one.
\v 14 But each person is tempted when he is drawn away and enticed by his own evil desires.
\v 15 And when desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin. And when sin is fully grown, it brings forth death.
\v 16 Do not be deceived, my beloved brothers.
\p
\v 17 Every good and perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, with whom there is no variation or shadow of turning.
\v 18 Of his own will he gave us birth by the word of truth so that we would be a kind of firstfruits of his creatures.
\s1 Hearing and Abiding by the Word
\m
\v 19 \f + \fr 1:19 \fq So then, my beloved brothers, everyone \ft ¦ Know this, my beloved brothers: Everyone CT\f*So then, my beloved brothers, everyone should be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to anger.
\v 20 For the anger of man does not produce the righteousness of God.
\v 21 Therefore, putting aside all filthiness and rampant wickedness, humbly receive the implanted word, which is able to save your souls.
\p
\v 22 But be doers of the word and not hearers only. Otherwise, you are deceiving yourselves.
\v 23 For if anyone is a hearer of the \f + \fr 1:23 \fq word \ft ¦ law PCK\f*word and not a doer, he is like a man who looks at his face in a mirror.
\v 24 For he looks at himself and goes away, and then immediately forgets what he looks like.
\v 25 But he who looks into the perfect law, the law of liberty, and abides by it, \f + \fr 1:25 \fq is not a forgetful hearer but a doer who acts. He \ft 87.1% ¦ is thus not a forgetful hearer but a doer who acts. He MSS 6.8% ¦ being not a forgetful hearer but a doer who acts, he CT 5.4%\f*is not a forgetful hearer but a doer who acts. He will be blessed in what he does.
\p
\v 26 If anyone \f + \fr 1:26 \fq among you \ft 88.3% ¦ — CT 9.4%\f*among you considers himself to be religious but does not bridle his tongue, he deceives himself and his religion is worthless.
\v 27 Pure and undefiled religion before God our Father is this: to look after orphans and widows in their affliction and to keep oneself unstained by the world.
\c 2
\s1 Warning Against Partiality
\m
\v 1 My brothers, practice your faith in our Lord Jesus Christ, the Lord of glory, without showing partiality.
\v 2 Suppose a man in fine clothing comes into your assembly wearing a gold ring, and a poor man in filthy clothing also comes in.
\v 3 If you pay attention to the man who is wearing fine clothing and say \f + \fr 2:3 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him, “Yoʋ sit here in a good place,” but you say to the poor man, “Yoʋ \f + \fr 2:3 \fq stand there, or sit here \ft 86.4% ¦ stand there, or sit TH 1% ¦ stand, or sit there NA SBL WH 1%\f*stand there, or sit here under my footstool,”
\v 4 have you not made distinctions among yourselves and become judges with evil thoughts?
\p
\v 5 Listen, my beloved brothers, has not God chosen the poor \f + \fr 2:5 \fq of the \ft 89.6% ¦ of this TR 6.1% ¦ in the CT 2.3%\f*of the world to be rich in faith and heirs of the kingdom that he has promised to those who love him?
\v 6 But you have dishonored the poor man. Is it not the rich who oppress you? Are they not the ones who drag you into court?
\v 7 Do they not blaspheme the good name by which you are called?
\v 8 If you really fulfill the royal law according to the Scripture, “Yoʋ shall love yoʋr neighbor as yoʋrself,” you do well.
\v 9 But if you show partiality, you commit sin and are convicted by the law as transgressors.
\v 10 For whoever keeps the entire law but stumbles in one point has become guilty of it all.
\v 11 For he who said, “\f + \fr 2:11 \fq Do … Do \ft ¦ Yoʋ shall … Yoʋ shall HF RP\f*Do not commit adultery,” also said, “Do not murder.” Now if yoʋ do not commit adultery but do commit murder, yoʋ have become a transgressor of the law.
\v 12 Speak and act as people who will be judged by the law of liberty.
\v 13 For judgment is without mercy to anyone who has not shown mercy; mercy \f + \fr 2:13 \fq is exalted \ft ¦ triumphs ANT CT TR\f*is exalted over judgment.
\s1 Faith and Works
\m
\v 14 What is the benefit, my brothers, if someone claims to have faith but does not have works? Can that faith save him?
\v 15 If a brother or sister is poorly clothed and lacks daily food,
\v 16 and one of you says to them, “Go in peace, be warmed and filled,” without giving them anything to address their physical needs, what is the benefit?
\v 17 In the same way faith by itself, if it does not have works, is dead.
\p
\v 18 But someone will say, “Yoʋ have faith, and I have works.” Show me yoʋr faith \f + \fr 2:18 \fq by \ft 87.7% ¦ apart from CT SCR 11.1%\f*by yoʋr works, and I, by my works, will show yoʋ my faith.
\v 19 Do yoʋ believe that God is one? Yoʋ do well; even the demons believe—and shudder!
\v 20 Do yoʋ want to be shown, O foolish man, that faith without works is \f + \fr 2:20 \fq dead \ft 98.2% ¦ useless CT 1.6%\f*dead?
\v 21 Was not our father Abraham justified by works when he offered his son Isaac on the altar?
\v 22 Do yoʋ see how faith was active along with his works, and by works his faith was brought to completion?
\v 23 And the Scripture was fulfilled that says, “Abraham believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness,” and he was called a friend of God.
\v 24 You see \f + \fr 2:24 \fq then \ft 86.9% ¦ — CT 11.7%\f*then that a person is justified by works, and not by faith alone.
\v 25 In the same way, was not Rahab the prostitute also justified by works when she received the messengers and sent them out by another way?
\v 26 \f + \fr 2:26 \fq For just \ft ¦ Just WH\f*For just as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is also dead. \fig Was not our father Abraham justified by works when he offered his son Isaac on the altar?|src="20 James 02_21.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="2:21" \fig*
\c 3
\s1 Taming the Tongue
\m
\v 1 Not many of you should become teachers, my brothers, because you know that we who teach will be judged more strictly.
\v 2 For we all stumble in many ways. If anyone does not stumble in what he says, he is a perfect man, able to bridle his entire body as well.
\v 3 \f + \fr 3:3 \fq Behold, we put bits into the mouths of horses so that they will obey us, and we guide their entire bodies. \ft ¦ If we put bits into the mouths of horses so that they will obey us, we guide their entire bodies as well. CT\f*Behold, we put bits into the mouths of horses so that they will obey us, and we guide their entire bodies.
\v 4 Or take ships for example, though they are so large and driven by fierce winds, they are guided by a very small rudder wherever the inclination of the helmsman directs.
\v 5 In the same way, the tongue is a small member, yet it makes great boasts.
\p Consider how great a forest \f + \fr 3:5 \fq a little \ft ¦ such a small CT\f*a little fire kindles.
\v 6 And the tongue is a fire. The tongue is placed among our members as a world of unrighteousness, staining the entire body, setting the course of life on fire, and itself being set on fire by hell.
\v 7 Every species of beast, bird, reptile, and sea creature can be tamed and has been tamed by man,
\v 8 but no man can tame the tongue. It is \f + \fr 3:8 \fq an unruly \ft 97.4% ¦ a restless CT 2.6%\f*an unruly evil, full of deadly poison.
\v 9 With it we bless \f + \fr 3:9 \fq God our \ft ¦ our Lord and CT\f*God our Father, and with it we curse men, who are made in God's likeness.
\v 10 Out of the same mouth come blessing and cursing. My brothers, this should not be.
\v 11 Does a spring pour out fresh water and bitter water from the same opening?
\v 12 Can a fig tree, my brothers, produce olives, or a grapevine figs? \f + \fr 3:12 \fq In the same way, no spring can produce both salt water and \ft ¦ Neither can salt water produce CT\f*In the same way, no spring can produce both salt water and fresh water.
\s1 The Wisdom from Above
\m
\v 13 Who is wise and understanding among you? By his good conduct he should show that his works are done with the gentleness that comes from wisdom.
\v 14 But if you have bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your hearts, do not boast or deny the truth.
\v 15 This is not the wisdom that comes down from above; rather, it is earthly, unspiritual, and demonic.
\v 16 For where there is jealousy and selfish ambition, there is disorder and every evil practice.
\v 17 But the wisdom from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, easily entreated, full of mercy and good fruits, impartial, and unhypocritical.
\v 18 And the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace.
\c 4
\s1 Friendship with the World
\m
\v 1 Where do wars and \f + \fr 4:1 \fq fights \ft ¦ where do fights CT\f*fights among you come from? Do they not come from your passions that wage war among your members?
\v 2 You desire but do not have. You murder and are jealous but cannot obtain what you want. You fight and war. \f + \fr 4:2 \fq You \ft ¦ And you \ft ANT ¦ Yet you TR\f*You do not have because you do not ask.
\v 3 You ask and do not receive because you ask wrongly, so that you may spend what you get on your pleasures.
\v 4 \f + \fr 4:4 \fq Adulterers and adulteresses \ft 97.8% ¦ Adulteresses CT 1.8%\f*Adulterers and adulteresses! Do you not know that friendship with the world is enmity with God? Therefore whoever wishes to be a friend of the world makes himself an enemy of God.
\v 5 Or do you think that the Scripture speaks in vain? The spirit that \f + \fr 4:5 \fq dwells \ft ¦ he made to dwell CT\f*dwells in us desires to the point of envy,
\v 6 but God gives greater grace. Therefore it says, “God opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble.”
\v 7 Submit yourselves therefore to \f + \fr 4:7 \fq God, but \ft ¦ God; ANT PCK TR\f*God, but resist the devil, and he will flee from you.
\v 8 Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you. Cleanse your hands, you sinners, and purify your hearts, you double-minded.
\v 9 Grieve, mourn, and weep. Turn your laughter into mourning and your joy into gloom.
\v 10 Humble yourselves before the Lord, and he will exalt you.
\s1 Speaking Evil Against One Another
\m
\v 11 Do not speak evil against one another, brothers. \f + \fr 4:11 \fq He \ft ¦ For he PCK\f*He who speaks evil against a brother \f + \fr 4:11 \fq and \ft 83.5% ¦ or CT 15.3%\f*and judges his brother, speaks evil against the law and judges the law. Now if yoʋ judge the law, yoʋ are not a doer of the law but a judge.
\v 12 There is only one \f + \fr 4:12 \fq lawgiver \ft ¦ lawgiver and judge ANT CT PCK SBL WH\f*lawgiver—he who is able to save and destroy. \f + \fr 4:12 \fq But who \ft ¦ Who TR\f*But who are yoʋ to judge \f + \fr 4:12 \fq another \ft ¦ yoʋr neighbor CT\f*another?
\s1 Boasting About Tomorrow
\m
\v 13 Come now, you who say, “Today \f + \fr 4:13 \fq and \ft ¦ or CT ELZ SCR\f*and tomorrow \f + \fr 4:13 \fq let us \ft ¦ we will ANT CT ELZ SCR\f*let us go to such and such a city, spend a year there, trade, and make a profit.”
\v 14 You do not know \f + \fr 4:14 \fq what will happen \ft ¦ \ft about WH\f*what will happen tomorrow. What is \f + \fr 4:14 \fq your \ft ¦ our PCK\f*your life? \f + \fr 4:14 \fq It is \ft ¦ You are CT {Note: The main Greek text literally reads \+it It will be\+it*.}\f*It is a vapor that appears for a little while and then vanishes away.
\v 15 Instead you ought to say, “If the Lord wills, \f + \fr 4:15 \fq let us live and \ft ¦ we will live and ANT CT SCR \ft \ft ¦ let us live, and we will\f*let us live and do this or that.”
\v 16 But as it is, you boast in your arrogant schemes; all such boasting is evil.
\v 17 So whoever knows the right thing to do and does not do it, for him it is sin.
\c 5
\s1 Warning to the Rich
\m
\v 1 Come now, you who are rich, weep and howl over the miseries that are coming upon you.
\v 2 Your riches have rotted, and your garments are moth-eaten.
\v 3 Your gold and silver have rusted, and their rust will be a testimony against you and will eat your flesh like fire. You have laid up treasure in the last days.
\v 4 Behold, the wages of the workers who harvested your fields, which you kept back by fraud, cry out, and the cries of the reapers have reached the ears of the Lord of hosts.
\v 5 You have lived on the earth in luxury and self-indulgence. You have nourished your hearts \f + \fr 5:5 \fq as \ft ¦ — CT\f*as in a day of slaughter.
\v 6 You have condemned and murdered the righteous man; he does not resist you.
\s1 Patience in Suffering
\m
\v 7 Therefore be patient, brothers, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, being patient for it until it receives the early and latter rain.
\v 8 You also must be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord has drawn near.
\v 9 Do not complain against one another, brothers, so that you will not be \f + \fr 5:9 \fq judged \ft ¦ condemned TR\f*judged. Behold, the Judge is standing at the door.
\v 10 As an example of suffering and patience, \f + \fr 5:10 \fq my \ft ¦ — CT PCK\f*my brothers, take the prophets, who spoke in the name of the Lord.
\v 11 Behold, we regard as blessed those who \f + \fr 5:11 \fq endure \ft ¦ have endured CT\f*endure. You have heard of the endurance of \f + \fr 5:11 \fq Job, and you have seen \ft ¦ Job. Now consider RP\f*Job, and you have seen the purpose of the Lord, that \f + \fr 5:11 \fq he \ft 87.7% ¦ the Lord ANT CT TR 11.3%\f*he is full of compassion and mercy.
\p
\v 12 Above all, my brothers, do not swear, neither by heaven, nor by earth, nor by any other oath. But let your “Yes” be “Yes” and your “No” be “No,” so that you will not fall \f + \fr 5:12 \fq into hypocrisy \ft ¦ under judgment CT ELZ SCR\f*into hypocrisy.
\s1 The Prayer of Faith
\m
\v 13 Is anyone among you suffering? He should pray. Is anyone of good cheer? He should sing praise.
\v 14 Is anyone among you sick? He should call for the elders of the church, and they should pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.
\v 15 The prayer of faith will save the one who is sick, and the Lord will raise him up. And if he has committed sins, he will be forgiven.
\v 16 \f + \fr 5:16 \fq Confess \ft ¦ Therefore confess CT\f*Confess your \f + \fr 5:16 \fq trespasses \ft 90.1% ¦ sins CT 9.7%\f*trespasses to one another and pray for one another so that you may be healed. The prayer of a righteous person is very powerful and effective.
\v 17 Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and he prayed earnestly that it would not rain, and for three years and six months it did not rain on the land.
\v 18 Then he prayed again, and the sky gave rain, and the land produced its fruit.
\p
\v 19 \f + \fr 5:19 \fq Brothers \ft ¦ My brothers CT\f*Brothers, if anyone among you wanders from the truth and someone turns him back,
\v 20 be assured that whoever turns a sinner back from the error of his way will save \f + \fr 5:20 \fq a \ft 90.9% ¦ his CT 5.7%\f*a soul from death and cover a multitude of sins.
